<!-- Stack of stacks -->
Since --- thanks to lift inference --- we always know how many markers on the stack we would have to skip in a canonical semantic representation, <!-- Canonical/Classical/...? -->
it is natural to represent the stack in a segmented fashion.
This way, instead of searching for the $k$th marker, we can "just" access the $k$th stack segment.
In effect<!-- i.e., in Effekt :) -->, we have a stack of stacks.
To distinguish the two levels, we call the outer level stack the "metastack", in analogy to metacontinuations 
[useful reference for metacontinuations, maybe some additional relation to them]{ .todo-style }.
Where canonical representations would push some marker on the stack, we start a new stack.
In notation, this looks quite similar if we annotate the stack bottom symbol with the marker.

<!-- Metastack operations ~ Evidence -->
In the presented calculus, the computations of evidence and the management of the metastack are mostly independent,
and this corresponds to the implementation.
However, they will always be closely related syntactically.
That is, there are two kinds of uses for $push stack$:
One corresponds to a $reset$ operation, and occurs together with a $new stack$ and the instantiation of matching evidence.
The other corresponds to a $resume$, and is directly followed by a $return$.
This restriction is not enforced by the implementation, which could allow for additional static optimizations. [ Really? ]{ .todo-style }

