Many of the abstract machine rules correspond directly to the RPython implementation.
For example, the rule

|:~~~~~~~~~~~|~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~:|~~~~~~~|~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~|~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~|
| _(new)_    | $< memory // x <- new V(\many{x_j}); stmt >$  | $-->$ | $< memory\{x \mapsto <V,\many{v_j}>\} // stmt >$ | if $v_j = memory(x_j)$    |
directly corresponds to the following code:
```Python
    elif isinstance(op, NEW):
        env.set_codata(op.out, CoData.make(op.args, env, op.vtable))
```

Looking at the rules
~ Small

|:~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~|~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~:|~~~~~~~|~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~|~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~|
| _(shift)_      | $< memory // x <- shift v; stmt // M>$ | $-->$ | $< memory // x <- shift v; stmt // \circ <-@ M>_{<-@}$ |
| _(unwind $there$)_     | $< memory // x <- shift (v\oplus there); stmt // M <-@ k ::: M'>_{<-@}$ | $-->$ | $< memory // x <- shift v; stmt // k ::: M <-@ M'>_{<-@}$ | <!-- Do we really want to formalize unwinding/rewinding directly? like this? -->
| _(unwind $here$)_   | $< memory // x <- shift here; stmt // M' <-@ k' ::: M>_{<-@}$ | $-->$ | $< memory\{ x \mapsto k' ::: M' \} // stmt // M>$ |
~
we have the following implementation:
```Python
    elif isinstance(op, SHIFT_DYN):
        n = promote(env.get_int(op.n))
        (cont, metastack) = split_metastack_n(MetaStack(stack, None, metastack), n+1)
        env.set_cont(op.out, cont)
        record_exact_value(metastack is not None, True)
        if metastack is not None:
            stack = metastack.stack
            metastack = metastack.tail
        else:
            stack = None
        context = get_context(pc_block, pc_instruction, stack, metastack)
```
This just merges _(unwind $here$)_ and _(unwind $there$)_ into the implementation
as a function call to make the code more readable.
Additionally, it provides the jit with the (statically ensured) invariant
that we will not try to capture more stack than we have.

