### Specializing by environment shape
~ TODO
Box and pointer diagrams?
~
Stack frames, data values, and codata values (i.e., objects) all internally
have to store an environment.
Since we cannot have RPython data types with inlined arrays,
and the sizes of those environments are not known at the compile time of the implementation, 
this results in allocations for $2$ arrays --- one for numbers and one for references.
This is undesirable for common cases like, e.g., the cons cells of a list.
Thus, for all of those, we have specialized variants for common cases, namely for:
~ TODO
List of how much we specialize
~

To prevent dynamic dispatch for those specialized versions, we declare the correct
specialization to the jit using program information.
We can easily get at those in all cases by 
caching the frame type by return program counter (for stacks),
caching codata sizes by virtual table (for codata), or
using the form of the match clause (for data).

### Additional JIT entry points
RPython requires us to call into the JIT at some program points in a way that ensures
that we do this at least once in every loop.
An easy heuristic is to do this on every (textually) backwards transfer of control.

For breaking all loops (and thus, all hot ones), it would be enough to just
allow entering the JIT on those control transfer operations.
However, for RPython's allocation removal to work well,
it is desirable to cut the loop *before* values are allocated and *after* they are used.
Thus, we additionally enter the jit after each `push` (which allocates a stack frame).

### Fake loops
RPython would usually detect a loop whenever it reaches the same (smaller) program location again,
in this case whenever we return to the same location.
If we now, e.g., call a library function twice in a row,
we will potentially trace a false loop that will never execute more than once.
This problem is well known for tracing jits [Reference]{.todo-style}.
To aleviate it, it is useful to consider some of the calling context.
Thus, we also consider the target of the topmost stack frame when determining the program location.
Note that, since we do not push for tail calls, this corresponds to a distinction about
the immediate 

